Overview
This challenge lab walks you through evaluation-driven optimization of an agent's harness. You are provided a minimalistic baseline coding agent and an evaluation with five tasks. You use that eval to diagnose why the agent fails and iteratively improve it until it succeeds on all tasks.

The model is deliberately fixed and small (gemini-3.1-flash-lite). With 'use a bigger model' off the table, every gain must come from the agent's tools and its system instructions. This lab tests your ability to analyze what the agent actually did, find why it failed, change the harness, and evaluate the improved version. Antigravity is your partner throughout.

What you'll do
In this lab, you perform the following tasks:

Run an eval that scores the agent on five coding tasks, graded by tests that are hidden from the agent.
Manually analyze agent trajectories - the exact tool calls it made and the code it shipped.
Automate trajectory analysis with Antigravity.
Use the findings to identify specific opportunities to improve the agent.
Improve the tools and system prompt in agent.py, and test the improved agent by rerunning the eval.
What the eval will show you: with the model fixed, the harness (tools, context, verification) is where the agent's capability is won or lost. The same model that solves almost nothing with a naive agent harness can succeed with an optimal harness.

Rules of this lab
The model never changes (gemini-3.1-flash-lite with LOW thinking budget).
You may only edit agent.py, where the tools and system instructions are defined.
You must never edit anything under tasks/, including task-specific prompts and tests.
Prerequisites
To successfully complete this challenge lab, you should be familiar with basic Python coding skills, command-line operations, AI agent concepts (tools, system prompts, trajectories), and root-cause analysis methodologies.

Please refer to the Design and optimization of coding agents lecture slides during the lab.

Structure of the lab
The files
path	what it is	may you edit it?
agent.py	The coding agent with 2 tools (read_file, write_file) and one-line system prompt. This is the only file you change.	Edit manually and/or with Antigravity
eval.py	The evaluation harness: loads the tasks, creates sandbox directories for them, runs the agent, and grades the results.	Read it but don't edit any parts of the eval harness
tasks/<id>/prompt.md	Short (one page or less) prompts that explain the task - describe an app that should to be built or a bug that should be fixed.	Do not edit
tasks/<id>/tests/hidden.py	The grader. Pass == exit 0. The agent never sees this script.	Do not edit
tasks/<id>/src/	Used only in "bug fix" tasks, provides the initial buggy code.	Do not edit
runs/<timestamp>/<id>/	Everything saved from a run. This is where you debug.	Analyze trajectories manually and with Antigravity
Evaluation Dataset includes two types of tasks
3 "Bug Fix" tasks have a src/ that contains a bug. The agent must find and fix the root cause.
4 "Build" tasks without src/. The agent writes a CLI app from scratch based on a spec.
What an eval run leaves behind
Every run writes all task-specific artifacts to runs/<timestamp>/<task_id>/.

artifact	contents
trajectory.evalset.json	The full trajectory in ADK format: every model turn, every tool call with its arguments, every tool result.
tool_uses.json	Short version of the trajectory showing all tool calls for quick skimming.
src/	The exact code generated by the agent. Review it as part of root cause analysis.
repro.sh	Shell script for re-running the same tests that the evaluation harness uses for scoring eval results. You can use it as a part of root cause analysis.
run.json	Eval run metadata: passed, tool_calls, seconds, and stop_reason
Before you start
Take a few minutes to review the initial implementation of agent.py. There are only 100 lines of code and it is critical that you understand the agent design before you start optimizing it. Also review the five tasks by reading their short prompts, and skip through the source code of eval.py to understand how the evaluation harness works. If anything looks confusing try asking Antigravity to answer any questions about this code for you.

