Task 3. Analyze agent trajectories manually
To effectively optimize the agent, you must first manually investigate the latest trajectory of a failed task before you start delegating trajectory analysis to Antigravity. This is critical for learning the concepts, so that you can effectively steer Antigravity through the same process in the next steps.

Manual root-cause analysis (optional): Select a failed task from the last baseline run (e.g., invoice_report). Open its run directory using the path printed in eval results. Execute the reproduction script to expose the failing assertions:
bash runs/<timestamp>/invoice_report/repro.sh
Copied!
Output:

Traceback (most recent call last):
  File "/tmp/tmp.lyaTkL5FV9/tests/hidden.py", line 8, in <module>
    assert invoice_total(amounts) == 125.49, invoice_total(amounts)
           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
AssertionError: 124.0
hidden.py exit code: 1 (0 = PASS)
Manually investigate the agent's trajectory (trajectory.evalset.json) in the Cloud Shell Editor and analyze the tool calls in order.

Your goal is to diagnose why the coding agent failed — the capability it was missing or the wrong move it made — not merely the code bug hidden in the task.

Submit your diagnosis for grading: This step grades your diagnosis of the agent, not the task. Write a brief explanation that covers:

the failing task you picked
what you observed the agent do wrong
why the coding agent failed to solve it — the specific capability it lacked or the wrong move it made (for example, it could not search the codebase to find the true source of the bug, or it never ran its own code to verify the fix)
A diagnosis that only restates the task's code bug will score low; you must explain the agent's gap. Submit it by running the Antigravity skill submit-task-diagnosis with the task name and your analysis, for example:

/submit-task-diagnosis For {exact_task_name}, I observed {brief description of key problems you found in the trajectory}; the coding agent failed because {the missing capability or wrong behavior}.
Copied!
The skill only accepts a task that actually failed, so choose a failing task. It grades your explanation against a rubric and records your submission only if it passes. If it scores below the bar, revise your analysis using the feedback and submit again.

Click Check my progress to verify the objective.
AT ID: 2 Analyze agent trajectories manually

Task 4. Scale trajectory analysis with Antigravity
Now that you have manually diagnosed one failure and understand the pattern, delegate the same investigation for the remaining failed tasks to the Antigravity CLI.

Switch to the Cloud Shell Terminal and submit a prompt instructing the CLI to analyze the remaining failures and trajectories in the @runs/<timestamp>/ directory for the agent defined in @agent.py.
Note: Your request must be a diagnosis only. Ensure the CLI does not edit any files during this analysis phase. You do not need to submit these automated findings—they feed directly into your optimization work in the next task.
Task 5. Optimize agent.py and validate
In this task, you apply your findings to improve the agent's capabilities and validate the changes.

Iteratively apply targeted improvements to the tools and system instructions within agent.py using the Antigravity CLI.

Re-run the evaluation harness (python eval.py or python eval.py --task <id>) to measure the impact of your changes.

Continue the measure-diagnose-improve loop until the agent achieves a pass rate of at least 4 out of 5 tasks on most runs.

Note: You must build solutions that generalize. Never hardcode answers, check for specific task IDs, or write logic that only solves one specific test case. If you overfit your agent to a specific task, your submission will automatically fail the final grading.
Click Check my progress to verify the objective.
AT ID: 3 Optimize the agent to solve at least four tasks

Submit the final version of your optimized agent for grading
